---
// Hero stat callout — the §10.4 "AI Attribution Hero" SHAPE (one plain-English headline plus
// 3-5 AI source chips), borrowed for the marketing hero and bounded by §26.
//
// ── WHY THERE IS NO SECOND SENTENCE ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
// §10.4's own example reads: "AI tools influenced 42 leads this month. ChatGPT and Perplexity
// were the strongest sources." The first sentence is a count. The SECOND ranks the sources and
// therefore reads as a narrated insight about the data — which is the shape §26 rules out
// (LLM analyzer, fake recommendations) and CLAUDE.md §6 rules out for good (no LLM-narrated
// freeform revenue/attribution numbers, marketing site included). So this ships the count and
// the source chips, and stops. The chips are a set, deliberately unordered and unranked; no
// chip is styled as "strongest", and there is no delta, arrow, or trend claim.
//
// Also absent by rule: no model version labels (§26), no CTA. §10.4's "View AI sources" CTA
// targets an in-app screen; the hero already carries two buttons, and a third pointing at a
// login wall would be worse than none.
//
// ── STATIC FIXTURE MARKUP (§29.5) ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Numbers and chip names are hardcoded below and nothing here reads a data source of any kind
// — no network access, no data client, no query helper, no real customer data. Same
// construction as DashboardMockup.astro / DirectRescueMockup.astro, which this sits directly
// above. The guard test greps this whole FILE for data-access tokens, comments included, so a
// commented-out one would be caught too — which is why none are spelled out literally here.
//
// DISCLOSURE IS THE INLINE LINE PLUS THE PAGE FOOTER, NEVER A PER-CARD BADGE. This component
// originally carried a "Sample data" pill. §29.8 — codified in #584 after this was written, from
// the pattern #581 shipped — bans per-card badges outright: one small muted footer line per page
// ("Product visuals shown use illustrative data.", Footer.astro:75) is the whole disclosure, and
// a pill competes with real content while reading as a defensive sticker.
//
// The "Illustrative example, not a customer's data" sentence below STAYS. That is not a badge and
// it is the same construction DirectRescueMockup uses, pinned by direct-rescue-mockup-fixture
// .test.js, which asserts the badge is absent AND that sentence is present. A specific figure in
// a hero is exactly what a visitor would otherwise read as a live measurement (§6).
// UPGRADED from a plain colour dot per chip to a real BrandMark tile. Claude and
// Gemini are officially sourced (marketing/src/lib/brand-marks.js); ChatGPT and
// Perplexity render as monogram tiles until sourcing lands for them (both 403 a
// bot fetch — recorded in the registry, not a gap in this component). Slugs, not
// display names: BrandMark resolves the label from the registry, so the chip
// text and the asset can never disagree about which company this is.
import BrandMark from "@/components/BrandMark.astro"

const LEAD_COUNT = 42;
const AI_SOURCES = ["chatgpt", "perplexity", "claude", "gemini"];
---

<div class="mx-auto mt-8 w-full max-w-[560px]">
  {/* Solid fill, no backdrop blur. `bg-[#121616]/80 backdrop-blur-xl` was
      glassmorphism, barred by §25.1 (open founder decision, binding on generation
      until ruled) — and this section was regenerated in Phase 2a, so "it was already
      here" does not exempt it. Legibility was checked before the change rather than
      after: composited over the hero's paper canvas the fill goes #404241 -> #121616,
      and contrast IMPROVES on both text pairs — white 10.13 -> 18.23, the #C7CFCF
      chips 5.52 -> 10.14. Opaque, not a substitute effect: no replacement blur,
      scrim, or shadow. */}
  <div class="rounded-[20px] border border-[#2D3333] bg-[#121616] px-5 py-4">
    <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
      <p class="text-left text-sm font-black tracking-tight text-white sm:text-base">
        AI tools influenced {LEAD_COUNT} leads this month
      </p>
    </div>

    <ul class="mt-3 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-2">
      {
        AI_SOURCES.map((slug) => (
          <li class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 rounded-full bg-white/5 px-2 py-1 text-[11px] font-bold text-[#C7CFCF]">
            <BrandMark slug={slug} size={16} showName class="bm--on-dark" />
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ul>

    <p class="mt-3 text-left text-[11px] leading-relaxed text-[#9DA7A7]">
      Illustrative example, not a customer's data. SourceTrack detects AI referrers that most
      analytics record as Direct.
    </p>
  </div>
</div>
